## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design cross-cloud IaC security policy gates using industry-standard tools (tfsec, Checkov); covers secret management, encryption, versioning, provider pinning, tagging, and backend security.

## Key Concepts
- Secrets in code and tfvars detection
- Cloud storage encryption and versioning
- Provider version pinning and backends
- Tagging and access controls across clouds
- CI integration and local verification for IaC gates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Failing: plaintext secret in Terraform
variable \"db_password\" { default = \"SuperSecret123\" }
resource \"aws_db_instance\" \"db\" {
  password = var.db_password
}

// Passing: secret retrieved from Secrets Manager
data \"aws_secretsmanager_secret_version\" \"db_pass\" {
  secret_id = \"arn:aws:secretsmanager:region:acct:secret:db-password\"
}
resource \"aws_db_instance\" \"db\" {
  password = data.aws_secretsmanager_secret_version.db_pass.secret_string
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you unit-test this gate locally? 
- How would you evolve checks for multi-cloud secret fetch patterns?

## Why This Is Asked
A practical beginner-level check for implementing and validating security controls in Kubernetes using policy engines, focusing on least privilege and safe defaults.

## Key Concepts
- Pod Security Standards Baseline/Restricted
- Policy engines: Kyverno, OPA Gatekeeper
- Privilege escalation controls: runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem, capabilities
- Testing methods: compliant vs non-compliant pods, namespace scoping
- Change management and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Conceptual Kyverno policy (JS object for illustration)
const policy = {
  apiVersion: 'kyverno/v1',
  kind: 'ClusterPolicy',
  metadata: { name: 'require-baseline-security' },
  spec: {
    rules: [
      {
        name: 'require-baseline',
        match: { resources: { kinds: ['Pod'] }},
        validate: {
          message: 'Pods must set runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem and avoid hostPath',
          pattern: {
            spec: {
              containers: { any: { securityContext: { runAsNonRoot: true, readOnlyRootFilesystem: true } }},
              hostPath: { absent: {} }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle exceptions for legitimate hostPath usage?
- How would you monitor for policy violations in production?
